\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces Primary Bayes-factor sensitivity to the bounce prior, evaluated for a mock SPHEREx detection at $f_{\rm  NL}=-4.375$ ($\sigma =0.7$) with the GR-contamination amplitude fixed (no GR marginalization), except row~4, which reports the GR-marginalization spread. ``BF vs.\ SSFSR'' evaluates standard single-field slow-roll as a point hypothesis at its parameter-free prediction $f_{\rm  NL}^{\rm  inf}\approx 0.015$ (effectively $f_{\rm  NL}=0$ at survey precision); the tabulated entries are exact closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) evaluations under the stated assumptions, but because their magnitudes are strongly prior-width and point-hypothesis dependent, they should be \emph  {interpreted} only at the order-of-magnitude level. The $\sigma _{\rm  theory}=1.0$ Gaussian row is the recommended physically motivated headline (BF~$\sim 10$ at broad multifield $[-15,+15]$, BF~$\sim 4$ at narrow $[-5,+5]$): it covers the full $\epsilon $-correction range within $1\sigma $, while the Li \textit  {et~al.}\ single-time-ordering value $-2.1875$ lies at $2.19\sigma $ under this prior (inside the $\sim 2\sigma $ tail, not within $1\sigma $; see Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}). The delta-prior row is reported only as the theoretical-maximum upper bound; any finite theoretical uncertainty in the $f_{\rm  NL}=-35/8$ value monotonically reduces the Bayes factor (Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}). $^a$The 4.7--7.0 spread on this row reflects the GR-marginalization variation across the scenarios of Table~\ref {tab:gr} at fixed \emph  {narrow} multifield competitor prior $[-5,+5]$; this is the same competitor prior that produces the $4.7$--$7.0$ ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column of Table~\ref {tab:gr}, and the two tables are numerically identical by construction (both from the closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) recompute, \texttt  {c9g\_bf\_table\_recompute.py}). The recommended $\sigma _{\rm  theory}=1.0$ headline of BF~$\sim 10$ is approximately constant under the same GR variation. $^b$The $\sim \!17$ row reports the delta-bounce-prior Bayes factor at the broad-multifield competitor prior $[-15,+15]$ with the GR-contamination amplitude fixed (no GR marginalization; $\sigma _{\rm  eff}=0.7$); GR marginalization at $\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$ reduces it to ${\sim }\,14$. This is the delta-prior endpoint of the competitor-prior sensitivity scan in Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}. The $r \to 1$ endpoint envelope $\sim \!10$--$17$ quoted in the abstract brackets the recommended-prior lower bound (BF~$\sim \!10$, row 1) up to the delta-prior maximum at broad multifield (BF~$\sim \!17$, row 5), with broader bounce priors giving \emph  {smaller} Bayes factors; the abstract headline $\mathrm  {BF} \approx 9$--$14$ applies the noise-weighted $r \approx 0.84$ bounce-amplitude bookkeeping to these endpoints (see the template-mismatch bookkeeping paragraph). }}{15}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bayes}{{II}{15}{Primary Bayes-factor sensitivity to the bounce prior, evaluated for a mock SPHEREx detection at $\fnl =-4.375$ ($\sigma =0.7$) with the GR-contamination amplitude fixed (no GR marginalization), except row~4, which reports the GR-marginalization spread. ``BF vs.\ SSFSR'' evaluates standard single-field slow-roll as a point hypothesis at its parameter-free prediction $\fnl ^{\rm inf}\approx 0.015$ (effectively $\fnl =0$ at survey precision); the tabulated entries are exact closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) evaluations under the stated assumptions, but because their magnitudes are strongly prior-width and point-hypothesis dependent, they should be \emph {interpreted} only at the order-of-magnitude level. The $\sigma _{\rm theory}=1.0$ Gaussian row is the recommended physically motivated headline (BF~$\sim 10$ at broad multifield $[-15,+15]$, BF~$\sim 4$ at narrow $[-5,+5]$): it covers the full $\epsilon $-correction range within $1\sigma $, while the Li \etal \ single-time-ordering value $-2.1875$ lies at $2.19\sigma $ under this prior (inside the $\sim 2\sigma $ tail, not within $1\sigma $; see Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}). The delta-prior row is reported only as the theoretical-maximum upper bound; any finite theoretical uncertainty in the $\fnl =-35/8$ value monotonically reduces the Bayes factor (Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}). $^a$The 4.7--7.0 spread on this row reflects the GR-marginalization variation across the scenarios of Table~\ref {tab:gr} at fixed \emph {narrow} multifield competitor prior $[-5,+5]$; this is the same competitor prior that produces the $4.7$--$7.0$ ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column of Table~\ref {tab:gr}, and the two tables are numerically identical by construction (both from the closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) recompute, \texttt {c9g\_bf\_table\_recompute.py}). The recommended $\sigma _{\rm theory}=1.0$ headline of BF~$\sim 10$ is approximately constant under the same GR variation. $^b$The $\sim \!17$ row reports the delta-bounce-prior Bayes factor at the broad-multifield competitor prior $[-15,+15]$ with the GR-contamination amplitude fixed (no GR marginalization; $\sigma _{\rm eff}=0.7$); GR marginalization at $\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$ reduces it to ${\sim }\,14$. This is the delta-prior endpoint of the competitor-prior sensitivity scan in Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}. The $r \to 1$ endpoint envelope $\sim \!10$--$17$ quoted in the abstract brackets the recommended-prior lower bound (BF~$\sim \!10$, row 1) up to the delta-prior maximum at broad multifield (BF~$\sim \!17$, row 5), with broader bounce priors giving \emph {smaller} Bayes factors; the abstract headline $\mathrm {BF} \approx 9$--$14$ applies the noise-weighted $r \approx 0.84$ bounce-amplitude bookkeeping to these endpoints (see the template-mismatch bookkeeping paragraph)}{table.2}{}}
