\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Left: $\sigma (f_{\rm  NL})$ as a function of $b_\phi $ prior uncertainty for MegaMapper SDB (blue). The SPHEREx bispectrum constraint (red dashed) is less sensitive to $b_\phi $ than SDB but not independent of it; the residual dependence enters at tree level through the $\Delta b(k) \propto f_{\rm  NL}\, b_\phi / k^2$ cross-terms $f_{\rm  NL}\, b_\phi \, b_1^2 P(k_1) P(k_2)$ in the multi-tracer Fisher matrix~\cite  {Heinrich:2023}, which propagate to all triangle configurations and not only the squeezed limit. Right: corresponding detection significance for $f_{\rm  NL}= -35/8$. At the paper's assumed 20\% prior (gray line; the SPHEREx multi-tracer bispectrum per-bin baseline is $\sigma (f_{\rm  NL}) = 0.7$ at this point), MegaMapper gives $\sim 4\sigma $; relaxing the per-bin $b_\phi $ prior to 30\% widens the per-bin SPHEREx $\sigma (f_{\rm  NL})$ to $\approx 0.9$--$1.0$, and relaxing to 50\% drops MegaMapper to $\sim 2\sigma $. The bispectrum channel remains at $\sim 5\sigma $ (optimistic) or $\sim 3$--$4\sigma $ (after GR degradation) at fixed Heinrich \textit  {et~al.}~$b_\phi $ universality~\cite  {Heinrich:2023}; relaxing $b_\phi $ universality per tracer bin~\cite  {Barreira:2022} degrades the optimistic $5.2$--$5.5\sigma $ headline to ${\sim }\,4.0$--$4.2\sigma $ ($30\%$ central) and ${\sim }\,3.5$--$3.7\sigma $ ($50\%$ conservative).}}{17}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bphi}{{5}{17}{Left: $\sigma (\fnl )$ as a function of $b_\phi $ prior uncertainty for MegaMapper SDB (blue). The SPHEREx bispectrum constraint (red dashed) is less sensitive to $b_\phi $ than SDB but not independent of it; the residual dependence enters at tree level through the $\Delta b(k) \propto \fnl \, b_\phi / k^2$ cross-terms $\fnl \, b_\phi \, b_1^2 P(k_1) P(k_2)$ in the multi-tracer Fisher matrix~\cite {Heinrich:2023}, which propagate to all triangle configurations and not only the squeezed limit. Right: corresponding detection significance for $\fnl = -35/8$. At the paper's assumed 20\% prior (gray line; the SPHEREx multi-tracer bispectrum per-bin baseline is $\sigma (\fnl ) = 0.7$ at this point), MegaMapper gives $\sim 4\sigma $; relaxing the per-bin $b_\phi $ prior to 30\% widens the per-bin SPHEREx $\sigma (\fnl )$ to $\approx 0.9$--$1.0$, and relaxing to 50\% drops MegaMapper to $\sim 2\sigma $. The bispectrum channel remains at $\sim 5\sigma $ (optimistic) or $\sim 3$--$4\sigma $ (after GR degradation) at fixed Heinrich \etal ~$b_\phi $ universality~\cite {Heinrich:2023}; relaxing $b_\phi $ universality per tracer bin~\cite {Barreira:2022} degrades the optimistic $5.2$--$5.5\sigma $ headline to ${\sim }\,4.0$--$4.2\sigma $ ($30\%$ central) and ${\sim }\,3.5$--$3.7\sigma $ ($50\%$ conservative)}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {III}{\ignorespaces Bayesian comparison with parameterized GR-contamination degradation. SSFSR = standard single-field slow-roll inflation. All Bayes factors are closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) evaluations at the central mock detection $\hat  f_{\rm  NL}=-35/8$ with the GR-contamination nuisance added in quadrature to the SPHEREx baseline, $\sigma _{\rm  eff}=\sqrt  {0.7^2+\sigma _{\rm  GR}^2}$; $P(\text  {BF}>3)$ is the fraction of $2\times 10^5$ mock-detection realizations $\hat  f_{\rm  NL}\sim \mathcal  {N}(-35/8,\sigma _{\rm  eff})$ exceeding BF~$=3$ against SSFSR (script and output released with the paper's code, \texttt  {c9g\_bf\_table\_recompute.py}; see Data and Code Availability; the binomial Monte-Carlo standard errors on the $P(\text  {BF}>3)$ column are $<0.1$ percentage points at $2\times 10^5$ realizations, below the quoted precision). The ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column reports the Bayes factor against a tuned multifield competitor with \emph  {narrow} prior $[-5,+5]$ at the delta bounce prior; the no-GR cell (7.0) coincides with the narrow$\to $broad sweep BF~$\sim 7\to 17$ at the delta bounce prior reported in the prose of \S  \ref {sec:bayesian}, and the corresponding broad $[-15,+15]$ competitor gives BF~$\sim 14$ at $\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$. The bounce-vs-inflation comparison survives all treatment scenarios. $^a$The ``Corrected (10\% residual)'' row is a bookkeeping verification row, not an independent configuration: it implements the limit in which GR contamination is corrected and the post-correction residual is neglected at this template-overlap order, so it equals ``Ideal'' by construction. For calibration, a literal residual nuisance of $\sigma _{\rm  GR} = 0.05$ (10\% of the $\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$ scenario amplitude surviving correction) gives $\sigma _{\rm  eff} = 0.702$ and BF vs.\ Tuned $= 6.99$ ($\Delta \text  {BF} = 0.01$, below the reported precision), while the exponentially $\sigma $-sensitive BF-vs-SSFSR column would read $3.1\times 10^8$ rather than $3.5\times 10^8$; the row is therefore reported in the strict zero-residual limit. The table thus contains three independent GR scenarios plus one verification row, and rows~1 and~4 bookend the same GR-free regime.}}{18}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:gr}{{III}{18}{Bayesian comparison with parameterized GR-contamination degradation. SSFSR = standard single-field slow-roll inflation. All Bayes factors are closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) evaluations at the central mock detection $\hat f_{\rm NL}=-35/8$ with the GR-contamination nuisance added in quadrature to the SPHEREx baseline, $\sigma _{\rm eff}=\sqrt {0.7^2+\sigma _{\rm GR}^2}$; $P(\text {BF}>3)$ is the fraction of $2\times 10^5$ mock-detection realizations $\hat f_{\rm NL}\sim \mathcal {N}(-35/8,\sigma _{\rm eff})$ exceeding BF~$=3$ against SSFSR (script and output released with the paper's code, \texttt {c9g\_bf\_table\_recompute.py}; see Data and Code Availability; the binomial Monte-Carlo standard errors on the $P(\text {BF}>3)$ column are $<0.1$ percentage points at $2\times 10^5$ realizations, below the quoted precision). The ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column reports the Bayes factor against a tuned multifield competitor with \emph {narrow} prior $[-5,+5]$ at the delta bounce prior; the no-GR cell (7.0) coincides with the narrow$\to $broad sweep BF~$\sim 7\to 17$ at the delta bounce prior reported in the prose of \S \ref {sec:bayesian}, and the corresponding broad $[-15,+15]$ competitor gives BF~$\sim 14$ at $\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$. The bounce-vs-inflation comparison survives all treatment scenarios. $^a$The ``Corrected (10\% residual)'' row is a bookkeeping verification row, not an independent configuration: it implements the limit in which GR contamination is corrected and the post-correction residual is neglected at this template-overlap order, so it equals ``Ideal'' by construction. For calibration, a literal residual nuisance of $\sigma _{\rm GR} = 0.05$ (10\% of the $\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$ scenario amplitude surviving correction) gives $\sigma _{\rm eff} = 0.702$ and BF vs.\ Tuned $= 6.99$ ($\Delta \text {BF} = 0.01$, below the reported precision), while the exponentially $\sigma $-sensitive BF-vs-SSFSR column would read $3.1\times 10^8$ rather than $3.5\times 10^8$; the row is therefore reported in the strict zero-residual limit. The table thus contains three independent GR scenarios plus one verification row, and rows~1 and~4 bookend the same GR-free regime}{table.3}{}}
