\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {IV}{\ignorespaces Consolidated systematic budget for the SPHEREx bispectrum detection of $f_{\rm  NL}= -35/8 = -4.375$. \textbf  {Row types:} the first row (naive uncorrected) is shown for reference only and is \emph  {not} used in any headline; the template-corrected baseline row ($r=0.84$) is the starting point for all subsequent rows; the $\epsilon $-correction and null-space rows are \emph  {distributional} contributions (not cumulative denominators); the $b_\phi $ and GR rows are \emph  {cumulative} additions to the effective denominator $\sigma _{\rm  eff}$; the all-combined rows stack both contributions simultaneously. ``Acts on'' refers to the signal numerator ($|f_{\rm  NL}| \times r$) or the effective denominator $\sigma _{\rm  eff}$. Combination rule ``$\oplus $'' denotes addition in quadrature ($\sigma _{\rm  eff} = \sqrt  {\sigma _{\rm  base}^2 + \sigma _{\rm  syst}^2}$), a transparent scoping choice described in Sec.~\ref {sec:assumptions} (additive-quadrature rather than joint-marginalized Fisher). Significance $= |f_{\rm  NL}| \times r / \sigma _{\rm  eff}$ with $r = 0.84$ (noise-weighted central value). The $b_\phi $-30\% row uses $\sigma (f_{\rm  NL}) \to 0.9$ (30\% widening over the baseline 0.7; the effective denominator increases to 0.9 as the $b_\phi $ marginalization replaces the baseline). The all-combined rows add GR degradation on top of the $b_\phi $-widened denominator using the additive-quadrature systematic budget defined in Sec.~\ref {sec:systematics}. The ${\sim }\,2.6$--$5\sigma $ post-systematic-budget envelope quoted in the abstract spans the all-combined conservative floor (bottom row) to the no-GR optimistic ceiling (template-corrected baseline row with $b_\phi $-20\% assumption). The effective denominator $\sigma _{\rm  eff}$ is defined in Sec.~\ref {sec:spherex} (Eq.~\ref {eq:projection}) as the quadrature combination of $\sigma (f_{\rm  NL}^{\rm  local})$ and any additional systematic nuisances; all $\sigma _{\rm  eff}$ values in this table follow that convention.}}{20}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:systematics}{{IV}{20}{Consolidated systematic budget for the SPHEREx bispectrum detection of $\fnl = -35/8 = -4.375$. \textbf {Row types:} the first row (naive uncorrected) is shown for reference only and is \emph {not} used in any headline; the template-corrected baseline row ($r=0.84$) is the starting point for all subsequent rows; the $\epsilon $-correction and null-space rows are \emph {distributional} contributions (not cumulative denominators); the $b_\phi $ and GR rows are \emph {cumulative} additions to the effective denominator $\sigma _{\rm eff}$; the all-combined rows stack both contributions simultaneously. ``Acts on'' refers to the signal numerator ($|\fnl | \times r$) or the effective denominator $\sigma _{\rm eff}$. Combination rule ``$\oplus $'' denotes addition in quadrature ($\sigma _{\rm eff} = \sqrt {\sigma _{\rm base}^2 + \sigma _{\rm syst}^2}$), a transparent scoping choice described in Sec.~\ref {sec:assumptions} (additive-quadrature rather than joint-marginalized Fisher). Significance $= |\fnl | \times r / \sigma _{\rm eff}$ with $r = 0.84$ (noise-weighted central value). The $b_\phi $-30\% row uses $\sigma (\fnl ) \to 0.9$ (30\% widening over the baseline 0.7; the effective denominator increases to 0.9 as the $b_\phi $ marginalization replaces the baseline). The all-combined rows add GR degradation on top of the $b_\phi $-widened denominator using the additive-quadrature systematic budget defined in Sec.~\ref {sec:systematics}. The ${\sim }\,2.6$--$5\sigma $ post-systematic-budget envelope quoted in the abstract spans the all-combined conservative floor (bottom row) to the no-GR optimistic ceiling (template-corrected baseline row with $b_\phi $-20\% assumption). The effective denominator $\sigma _{\rm eff}$ is defined in Sec.~\ref {sec:spherex} (Eq.~\ref {eq:projection}) as the quadrature combination of $\sigma (\fnl ^{\rm local})$ and any additional systematic nuisances; all $\sigma _{\rm eff}$ values in this table follow that convention}{table.4}{}}
